# swarm Provider

The swarm provider manages [Docker Swarm](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/) clusters from Terraform or OpenTofu. A [`swarm_cluster`](resources/cluster) is one cluster and its nodes: each apply reaches the nodes' Docker, adopts the cluster their managers are in or inits one, and sets each node's role, labels and availability. The data source [`swarm_cluster`](data-sources/cluster) reads a cluster from one of its managers.

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

## What you need

- **The nodes' Docker**, reachable from the machine Terraform runs on: `tcp://` in mTLS, `ssh://`, or a local socket. The machines come from the cloud's provider, which gives `swarm_cluster` their endpoints: they may be unknown until the same apply creates the machines, and the provider waits until each daemon answers.
- **Nothing to configure in the provider**: each node gives its own endpoint, so one configuration can manage several clusters.

## Compatibility

| | Version |
|---|---|
| Terraform | 1.0 or later; 1.12 or later to import by identity |
| OpenTofu | 1.6 or later |
| Docker Engine on the nodes | any with Swarm mode |

The provider is published on the [Terraform Registry](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/sntns/swarm) as `sntns/swarm`.

## What it leaves to other tools

Services, stacks, configs, secrets and networks belong to the tool that deploys what runs on the cluster; the machines, to the cloud's provider.

